## Comprehensive Summary
Monitoring of sweat pH plays important roles in physiological health, nutritional balance, psychological stress, and sports performance. However, the combination of functional MOFs with phosphorescent material to acquire the real-time physiological information, as well as the application of dual mode anti-counterfeiting, has seldom been reported. Herein, we developed multifunctional gel films based on MOFs and phosphorescent dyes which responded to ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ ions and the related mechanism was studied in detail. Upon exposure to ${\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ , the composite gel film exhibited decreased fluorescent signal but enhanced room temperature phosphorescence (RTP), which could be utilized for sweat pH sensing through a dual-mode. Moreover, multifunctional gel films exhibited a potential application in information encryption and anti-counterfeiting by designing of stimulus responsive multiple patterns. This research provided a new avenue for portable and non-invasive sweat pH monitoring methods while also offering insights into stimulus-responsive multifunctional materials.
## Background and Originality Content
Sweat not only removes heat but also helps excrete other chemicals and metabolites from the body. The lactate concentration of sweat, which plays an important role in sweat pH, depends on metabolism and physical activity, which links to energy metabolism for assessing fatigue. Besides, it can also be a potential biomarker for pressure ischemia, which leads to a shortage of oxygen that is needed for cellular metabolism.[1] Sweat pH beyond the usual range $(4-7)$ may indicate various health issues,[2] thus, monitoring of sweat pH plays important roles in physiological health, nutritional balance, psychological stress, and sports performance.[3-7] Thus far, various non-invasive point-of-care-testing sensing systems based on enzymes have been developed, however, some disadvantages including high cost, poor stability and susceptibilities to environmental influences limit their practical applications.[8] Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are potential candidates for non-enzymatic biosensing due to their unique porous structure, high stability and tailored functionalities.[9-13] The integration of functional MOFs with wearable sensors provides important insights into noninvasive technologies to acquire real-time physiological information.[14-18] However, the direct monitoring of sweat pH based on MOF fluorescence sensor has seldom been reported.
Recently, there has been a remarkable increase in the rational design of biosensor based on time-resolved emission characteristics. RTP with persistent luminescence, is more desired, owing to the disadvantages of the bare fluorescence probes including photo-bleaching, fluctuation of the excitation power, and so on.[19-21] It is more desired that the fluorescent and phosphorescent dyes exhibited different emission wavelength and opposite stimuli-responsive behaviour, thus, the relative changes in fluorescent and phosphorescent intensity might be utilized to monitor the sweat pH. Moreover, the combination of fluorescent and phosphorescent materials to construct dual-mode response provides new opportunities for anti-counterfeiting technology and practical application.[22-26] Information security protection has a tremendous impact on human life, social stability and national security, leading to the rapid development of anti-counterfeiting materials including perovskite phosphors,[27] quantum dots, organic dyes,[28] nanoparticles,[29] smart fluorescence polymer,[30-31] and so on. However, to the best of our knowledge, the studies about using sweat, a common humor, as the developer material have seldom been reported. In this work, the integration of DBCZ and PVA matrix can achieve long-lived RTP, and dual mode anti-counterfeiting images could be developed upon addition of the sweat. In this regard, it still remains a challenge to develop MOF-based functional composite materials with efficient dual-mode fluorescent and phosphorescent emission for sweat pH sensing and information encryption.
pH sensing behaviors of Y(III)-TCPP MOFs have been reported previously, and the pH response range of Y-TCPP MOF was from ${\mathsf{p H}}=6$ to ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ , which made the Y-TCPP MOF materials unsuitable for $\mathsf{p H}$ monitoring of sweat (around ${\mathsf{p H}}=5^{\prime}$ ) directly. By employing DBCZ and treating with NaOH solution firstly, the hybrid films not only exhibited pH-responsive behavior under acidic condition, but also exhibited long-lived RTP and delayed fluorescence which could be easily detected even by the naked eyes. Moreover, to the best of our knowledge, the studies about using sweat, a common body fluid, as the developer material for anti-counterfeiting have seldom been reported.
Herein, we prepared multifunctional composite gel films by the combination of fluorescent MOF and phosphorescent dyes. Under the influence of $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ , the composite films exhibited decreased fluorescent signal but enhanced RTP, which could be utilized for sweat pH sensing through a dual-mode. In addition, the above multifunctional composite gel films with pH-responsive fluorescence and RTP using sweat as the novel developer material exhibited a potential in information encryption and anti-counterfeiting application. This work not only provides in-depth understanding of the stimulus-responsive mechanism of MOF-based functional materials, but also advances their potential applications toward sweat pH sensing and anti-counterfeiting.
## Results and Discussion
Firstly, Y-TCPP MOF was synthesized according to the previous report,[32] as shown in Figure 1a. The successful synthesis of Y-TCPP MOF was confirmed by scanning electron microscopy-X-ray energy dispersion spectroscopy (SEM-EDS, Figure S1), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Figure S2) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, Figure S3). According to the SEM-EDS and XPS spectra, the existence of C, N, O and Y was substantiated. The characteristic peaks observed at 158.2 eV, 284.8 eV, $400.0\:\mathrm{eV},$ , and 532.0 eV in the XPS spectrum corresponded to the Y3d, C1s, N1s, and O1s peaks of Y-TCPP MOF, respectively. Additionally, the obtained powder of Y-TCPP MOF exhibited three distinct peaks in the small angle region (at 5.82, 7.46 and 8.88), consistent with previous literature,[32] as shown in the Figure S3, confirming the successful formation of MOFs. Moreover, the disk-like structure of Y-TCPP MOF could be clearly observed through SEM images, and the size of multiple disk structures stacked together is about $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Based on these results, Y-TCPP MOF composed of porphyrin ligands coordinated with Yttrium clusters could be considered successfully synthesized. The coordination ability offered by Yttrium distinguished this material from non-transition metal-based MOFs.
Y-TCPP MOF could emit the red fluorescence under $365~\mathsf{n m}$ irradiation under alkaline condition,[32] while had no photoluminescent properties in the pH range of sweat. On the other hand, 7H-dibenzo[c,g]carbazole (DBCZ)[33] emitted blue fluorescence and yellow-green RTP under $365~\mathsf{n m}$ excitation. According to the energy level diagrams of similar molecules and the previous reports,[33-35] the RTP emission for DBCZ might be ascribed to the transition from ${\sf T}_{1}$ to $\mathsf{S}_{0}$ .
The combination of the two luminous substances to prepare a multifunctional gel film brought the unexpected pH response properties in the pH range of sweat. Weakly acidic sweat could induce a decrease in the red fluorescence of MOF in the composite gel film, forcing the blue fluorescence of BDCZ to highlight, resulting in a red-blue transformation of the functional gel film visible to the naked eye. Besides, sweat could promote the phosphorescence of DBCZ. Based on these, a dual-mode sweat pH sensor and sweat-based anti-counterfeiting could be designed (Figure 1b).
## Properties of Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA composite film
According to the previous reports, the fluorescence intensity of Y-TCPP MOF increased with the increase of pH from 6 to 12.[32] Although these properties were still maintained in the composite film, there was no responsive photoluminescent property in the pH range of sweat. Actually, this excellent alkali stimulation responsive luminescence performance of MOF provided an excellent platform for our composite luminescent materials.
Fortunately, the multifunctional gel was successfully prepared by compositing the PVA with MOF and DBCZ. We have evaluated various concentrations of MOF and DBCZ, and determined that a ratio of $10~\mathsf{m g}$ MOF and 2 mg DBCZ per $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{g P V A}$ was suitable for achieving both strong fluorescence intensity and distinct blue fluorescence under neutral conditions, as well as red fluorescence under alkaline conditions. The composite film was constructed using the drop film method, and the required composite gel film can be obtained by evenly coating and drying. The presence of DBCZ and MOF could be observed through UV-Vis absorption spectroscopy (Figure S4a). It is worth noting that PVA was specially treated. Commercially available PVA was highly susceptible to water erosion without exception although we have attempted many times, which undoubtedly hinders the construction of composite materials. Fortunately, choosing the method of glutaraldehyde crosslinking to improve the water erosion resistance of PVA could solve this problem. At the same time, considering the crosslinking efficiency and the influence of PVA embrittlement resulting from the concentration is too high, the concentration of glutaraldehyde in the crosslinking solution was $1.25\%$ . It was confirmed that the film was completely immersed in the cross-linking solution (acetone : concentrated hydrochloric acid : $25\%$ glutaraldehyde $=52:5:3!$ ) for $5\min$ , there was no obvious water erosion on the gel film. The absorption spectra before and after crosslinking were measured, and the strong absorption at $370~\mathsf{n m}$ should be ascribed to the absorption of the DBCZ, while the abroad absorption in the visible region should be ascribed to the absorption of the MOF (Figure S4b). In addition, there was no obvious change of surface morphology before and after treatment through microscope photos (Figure S5). In order to further characterize the success of crosslinking, the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy was carried out before and after crosslinking. The C—O—C stretching vibration at $1142~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ was clearly visible, demonstrating the formation of acetals (Figure S6). Based on above, stimulus responsive films with good water resistance and specific response ability have been successfully synthesized. Next, we will discuss the coexistence adopted by MOF and DBCZ. As reported previously, if dye molecules were encapsulated into the MOFs channel structure, the fluorescence of the composite materials would be enhanced.[36] No obvious fluorescence enhancement could be observed for our hybrid films (Figure S7). In addition, if DBCZ acting as second ligands coordinates with Y, obvious shift in absorption spectrum would be observed.[37] However, no obvious changes could be observed for the composite samples (Figure S4a). All above results suggested that MOF and DBCZ adopted mainly disordered physical mixing in PVA films.
## Fluorescence behavior of Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA gel film at different pH values
The luminous properties of composite material were the key to the design of multifunctional film. The emission of Y-TCPP/ DBCZ PVA gel film with pH changes was recorded under $365~\mathsf{n m}$ excitation. The fluorescence of DBCZ remained basically unchanged at pH greater than 6 (Figure S8), while the fluorescence intensity of MOF increased with the increase of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ concentration as previously reported (Figure S9). The mechanism of fluorescence enhancement of MOF was attributed to the photo induced electron transfer (PET) (Figure 2a). It was worth noting that the MOF emission was obviously changed until the pH was up to 13 (Figure S10). The fluorescence photos were corresponding well to the results of spectroscopy. The blue fluorescence was observed at ${\mathsf{p}}{\mathsf{H}}=6{-}12$ , and turned to red when pH became 13, which might be due to that the weak fluorescence intensity of MOF at pH of $_{6-12}$ and the blue fluorescence emission of DBCZ was dominant. When ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ , prohibited PET resulted in the red fluorescence emission of the film (Figure 2b), thus, red was more visible to the naked eye. All above experimental results indicated that there was a mutation in the fluorescence color of the composite film from pH 12 to 13.
In order to further improve the application value of the composite material, the repeatability of the luminescence intensity caused by pH was necessary. However, immersing a red film with ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ into a Tris solution with ${\mathsf{p H}}=6$ did not restore the blue color. We believed that this might be due to the excessive amount of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{OH}}^{-}$ adsorbed in the film which was not completely neutralized. For this reason, the idea of using acids with a lower pH to neutralize excessive alkalis was proposed. However, the acetal structure was susceptible to hydrolysis in the acid, leading to significant degradation of film itself. Fortunately, the crosslinking solution itself exhibited a strong acidic nature, enabling effective neutralization of alkali. After putting the film with ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ into the crosslinking solution, the fluorescence of film quickly turned blue in less than $1\mathsf{m i n}$ , and the morphology of the film did not change obviously. Besides, in the repeatability experiment characterized by fluorescence intensity, the fluorescence intensity of gel film showed good repeatability in pH modulation of ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ solution and cross-linking solution (Figure 2c). The emission wavelength for red and blue emission was about 650 and $395\mathsf{n m}$ , respectively.
## Phosphorescence behavior of Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA mixed gel at different pH values
RTP had a wide range of applications in multi-field. There was no obvious difference between the phosphorescence spectra of the composite film and the DBCZ film (Figure S11). In order to investigate the phosphorescence property of the composite films, the effect of pH value on the yellow-green phosphorescence of DBCZ-MOF PVA composite films was detailedly studied. Phosphorescence could hardly be observed in wet films, which was consistent with reports that water can quench phosphorescence.[38] In this case, the films were immersed in Tris solution at ${\mathsf{p H}}=6$ to 13 for 10 min and well dried at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ subsequently, they were tested. Surprisingly, the phosphorescence of the dried film showed no discernible change within the pH range of 6 to 12, while exhibiting a pronounced leap in phosphorescent intensity between ${\mathsf{p H}}=12$ and 13, akin to the previously discussed phenomenon of fluorescence. When the pH increased to 13, the phosphorescence intensity of the film decreased obviously, and a porphyrin emission peak $(650\mathsf{n m})$ appeared, which was generally considered delayed fluorescence (Figure 3a). This phenomenon was attributed to energy transfer (Figure 3b). Indeed, for MOF film, the fluorescence lifetime was about 7 ns, and no obvious phosphorescence (the delay of 0.1 s) could be detected. As for the sample with the composite film, not only the phosphorescence at 520 and $540~\mathsf{n m}$ from DBCZ, but also the delayed fluorescence at about $650~\mathsf{n m}$ from MOF could be detected, as shown in Figure S12. All above results suggested the energy transfer from DBCZ to MOF.
The changes in phosphorescence could be clearly observed in the macroscopic photos of the composite film under different states. Actually, phosphorescence behaviors of Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA mixed gel at different pH values were studied in detail (Figure 3c), and the emission intensity of the film with ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ showed an obvious decrease compared to the film with ${\mathsf{p H}}=7$ (Figure 3d). The composite film exhibited blue fluorescence and yellow-green RTP at ${\mathsf{p H}}=6-12$ . When the pH rose to 13, the fluorescence of the film turned red, and the RTP intensity obviously decreased. Obviously, the phosphorescence intensity decreased more rapidly at ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ , especially at the beginning stage.
## Monitoring of sweat pH
In human body fluids, sweat was a weakly acidic liquid (pH $_{4-7)}$ with a wider pH range compared to other body fluids, such as blood. Sweat pH sensor could be used to diagnose metabolic alkalosis, which was a metabolic disease. When the pH value of tissue was beyond the normal range, it would lead to various health problems, such as arrhythmia, abnormal sensation, neuromuscular irritability, etc., and even Seizure and coma in severe cases. Inspired by the photoluminescence behaviour of the pH responsive composite film, a method for monitoring of sweat pH was proposed here.
Lactic acid played an important role in the pH of sweat. The discussion about the effect of lactic acid on the luminescence of the composite film was necessary. Considering the weak acidity of sweat itself and the luminescence change of gel film under alkaline conditions, the gel film was soaked in Tris solution with ${\mathsf{p H}}=$ 13 and then dried. Four common substances in sweat, lactic acid, glucose, NaCl, and urea, were selected to investigate the response selectivity of the gel film. $50~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}~10~\mathsf{m m o l}{\cdot\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ solution of selected substance was applied to the film and dried. Compared with the original state, the film with lactic acid treatment showed the most obvious fluorescence attenuation, while urea had little effect. The effects of glucose and NaCl on the fluorescence of the composite film were almost invisible (Figure 4a). These results demonstrated the selective response towards lactic acid for the composite film.
In order to investigate the effect of lactic acid on the luminescence of the gel films, further research was needed on response time and detection limit. Add $10~\upmu\upiota$ lactic acid solution of 10 mmol ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ onto the gel film and then record the fluorescence intensity. It was obvious that the fluorescence intensity remained basically unchanged after 6 min (Figure 4b). From the relationship diagram of lactate concentration and the luminescence intensity of the gel film, the fluorescence intensity decreased obviously after the lactate concentration exceeded 3 mmol, while the phosphorescence intensity increased obviously (Figure 4c). The above results indicated that the composite gel film could be applied to the detection of lactic acid in terms of fluorescent changes.
Based on the lactic acid detection discussed above, a sweat pH sensor based on Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA composite gel film was designed, as shown in Figure 5. The monotonic decrease of the fluorescence could be directly used for sweat monitoring. In order to ensure the repeatability of the experiment, artificial sweat was selected as the test sample to avoid the difference in pH. Artificial sweat with ${\mathsf{p H}}=4.7$ was selected as the base sample. After $50~\upmu\up L$ sweat with different content was applied to the film and dried, a linear relationship between the relative fluorescence intensities and the content of sweat $({\mathsf{p H}}=4.7)$ was observed at low concentrations of sweat, indicating that the above composite film could be applied to accurately monitor the content of sweat in aqueous solution. The detection limit $(3\sigma/S)$ was about $0.05\%$ (Figure S13). It was worth noting that the dissolved carbon dioxide in the solution might affect the luminescence of the film in multiple experiments. For higher concentrations of sweat, a decrease in fluorescence intensity was observed when the sweat content was below $3\%,$ while the phosphorescence intensity remained relatively unchanged. However, upon increasing of the sweat content above $10\%$ , the composite film exhibited apparent increase in the phosphorescence intensity, but the variation for the fluorescence intensity was not detectable, as shown in Figure 5a. Therefore, it was more reasonable to use the signal-ratio of the fluorescence and phosphorescence intensity for sweat monitoring, as shown in Figure S14. We also investigated the effect of coating volume on the luminescence of the composite gel films. The higher the volume of the sweat applied at, the lower the fluorescence intensity, and the stronger the phosphorescence intensity. However, due to the different sizes and shapes of sweat stains after drying, the differences were not significant (Figure 5b). Next, the response time of the composite film to sweat should be studied. $10~\upmu\upiota$ of sweat was dropped onto the film, and the time-dependent fluorescence intensities were recorded by analyzing the lightness of the fluorescence image by CCD. As shown in Figure 5c, the fluorescence intensity of the composite films exhibited an exponential decrease and finally remained almost unchanged over 16 min. Finally, the effects of artificial sweat with different pH on the luminescence of composite films were explored to expand the application of coincidence membranes in health diagnosis. To diminish the possible difference between different film samples or different batches, we used the intensity ratio of the luminescence intensity of the same composite film before and after sweat treatment to monitor the sweat pH. The higher the pH value, the smaller the change in luminescence intensity (Figure 5d). From the above results, a sweat pH sensor has been successfully constructed based on Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA composite gel film. This composite material has application prospects in body fluid detection and health examination.
## Dual-mode anti-counterfeiting
With the development of information technology and economy, the demand for anti-counterfeiting technology was becoming increasingly urgent in many fields such as food, medicine, clothing, and communication. The most common single color anti-counterfeiting was limited due to its single mode practical application. Herein, we presented a multimode anti-counterfeiting film using sweat as the novel developer material based on Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA composite gel film (Figure 6a). Considering the potential risk of dispersion of MOF in aqueous environments, writing with PVA dispersion of DBCZ and MOF was more suitable than applying MOF and DBCZ onto PVA films. Multifunctional gel 1 (a dispersion of 1 mg MOF, $100\mathrm{mg}\mathsf{P V},$ and $4~\mathsf{m L}$ water), gel 2 (a dispersion of $0.2~\mathsf{m g}$ DBCZ, $100\mathrm{~mg~PVA},$ and $4~\mathsf{m L}$ water), and gel 3 (a dispersion of 1 mg MOF, $0.2~\mathsf{m g}$ DBCZ, $100~{\mathsf{m g}}~{\mathsf{P V A}}.$ , and $4~\mathsf{m L}$ water) were prepared as ink for writing. Afterwards, the functional gels 1, 2 and 3 were used to write the numbers "1", "3" and $"6"$ on the left, middle and right of the slide, respectively. After drying and cross-linking, the composite gel film was immersed in a Tris solution with ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ . After the fluorescence of $"6"$ turns red, excess alkaline solution was wiped off and dried. The left of the slide showed a clear red "1", the middle showed a clear blue $"3"$ , and the right showed a clear rose red $"6"$ under the excitation of 365 nm. After turning off the ultraviolet light, a yellow green "3" in the middle of the slide and a faintly visible $"6"$ on the right could be seen. After soaking the film in an artificial sweat at ${\mathsf{p H}}=4.7$ for 20 min and drying, the left of the slide showed a light red "1", the middle showed a blue $"3"$ , and the right showed a light purple $"6"$ under the excitation of $365\ \mathsf{n m}$ . After turning off the ultraviolet light, the yellow green $"3"$ displayed in the middle remained unchanged from the ${}^{\prime\prime}3^{\prime\prime}$ before sweat treatment, while the weaker lightness $"6"$ on the right was clearly visible, and the luminous intensity is obviously higher than that before sweat treatment, as shown in Figure 6b. Due to the excessive adsorption of alkali by the composite film, the red emission of porphyrins could only completely disappear under strong acidic conditions. The sweat was weakly acidic which was not sufficient to eliminate the red fluorescence, thus, light red $^{\prime\prime}{}_{1}\ '{}$ was still visible. These experimental results demonstrate the application prospect of Y-TCPP/ DBCZ PVA mixed gel in dual-mode anti-counterfeiting materials, and show the application value of organic afterglow materials with adjustable solid emission in anti-counterfeiting.
## Conclusions
Based on the advantage of Y-TCPP MOF for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ specific response, a two-color stimulus response gel was constructed in combination with DBCZ PVA system, and a sweat pH sensing and anti-counterfeiting mode that was economical, applicable, easy to prepare and operate was found. A MOF with specific response to $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ was synthesized and combined with the DBCZ PVA system to construct a stimulus responsive material with red-blue color changing of fluorescence with pH, which has good repeatability. The composite film exhibited a sudden decrease in phosphorescence intensity at ${\mathsf{p H}}=13$ . The above spectroscopy results were visible to the naked eye. A sweat pH sensor based on composite film was constructed and exhibited different luminescence changes under different conditions. The luminescence response to sweat suggests the potential application of the composite film in health testing. Additionally, a dual-mode anti-counterfeiting material with both fluorescent and phosphorescent properties has been developed. Under alkaline and sweat conditions, it exhibited different fluorescence and phosphorescence images which were visible to the naked eye.
## Experimental
## Materials
Yttrium nitrate hexahydrate $(\Upsilon({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}},$ $99.9\%$ ), 7H-dibenzo[c,g]carbazole (DBCZ, $98\%$ ), polyvinyl alcohol 1799 (PVA, 1799 type, alcoholysis degree $98\%-99\%)$ , and lactate $(90\%)$ were purchased from Aladdin Co., Ltd. Tetra- $4$ -carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP, $95\%$ ) was purchased from Leyan Co., Ltd. Glutaraldehyde $25\%$ aqueous solution), hydrochloric acid $(36\%-38\%$ HCl aqueous solution), urea $(99.0\%)$ , glucose (AR), tris-(hydroxymethyl)- aminomethane (Tris, $99.5\%$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $99\%$ ), and sodium chloride (NaCl, $99.5\%$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Artificial sweat was purchased from Shenzhen Zhongwei Equipment Co., Ltd. All other solvents and reagents were of analytical grade and used as received. Milli-Q water $(18.2\mathsf{M}\Omega\cdot\mathsf{c m})$ was used in all cases.
## Instruments
GeminiSEM 500 was used to record the scanning electron microscope (SEM) image and the X-ray energy dispersion spectrum (EDS) image. The steady-state fluorescence and phosphorescence measurements were obtained on the Hitachi F-4600 fluorescence spectrophotometer at room temperature. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed on ESCALAB 250Xi X-ray photoelectron spectrometer. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was performed on the Rigaku SmartLab 9kW. SHIMADZU UV-2700 PC spectrophotometer was used to measure UV-vis absorption spectrum. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) was measured by using BRUKER TENSOR II.
## Preparation of Y-TCPP MOF
The synthesis method was taken from the previous report.[32] TCPP $21.0{\mathrm{~mg}}$ , 0.0266 mmol) was completely dissolved in DMF $(45.0\mathsf{m L})$ in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ round bottom flask. $\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $\scriptstyle\left.15.0\ m{\bf g},\right.$
$0.0392~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ) was dissolved in $3m L$ ultra-pure water. Then $\mathsf{Y}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ solution was added into TCPP solution and stirred evenly. The mixture was stirred and heated to $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $2h$ and further heated to $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2\ h$ . After cooling, the reacted liquid was centrifuged at $10000~\mathrm{r/mim}$ for $25\mathrm{\min}$ , the precipitation was collected, and washed twice with ethanol to obtain brown solid. The brown product attached to the bottle wall could be dispersed by soaking in anhydrous ethanol. Finally, Y-TCPP was stored in ethanol and needed to be shaken to uniform dispersion before use. The concentration of the dispersion solution was obtained by measuring a quantitative liquid in a bottle and weighing the solid after drying in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathsf{h}$ .
## Preparation of Y-TCPP/DBCZ PVA mixed gel
$100~\mathsf{m g}$ of PVA was added into a glass bottle containing $4~\mathsf{m L}$ of ultra-pure water, and heated at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ until it was completely dissolved. After cooling, $1\ \mathsf{m g}$ of centrifuged Y-TCPP MOF was added and the mixed solution was ultrasonicated until well-distributed. 1 mg of DBCZ was dissolved in $0.1~\mathsf{m L}$ of ethanol and ultrasonicated until it was fully dissolved. $20\upmu\upiota$ DBCZ solution was added to PVA solution and stirred for $0.5{\hslash}$ . The dispersion solution is light brown and unobvious turbidity was observed. $0.1~\mathsf{m L}$ of sample was dropped onto a glass sheet and dried in air at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $1\mathfrak{h}$ to form the gel film.
## Preparation of water-resistant mixed gel
$0.15~\mathsf{m L}$ of $25\%$ glutaraldehyde, $0.25~\mathsf{m L}$ of concentrated hydrochloric acid and $2.6~\mathsf{m L}$ of acetone were stirred evenly as the cross-linking solution. The gel film was completely immersed in a $5m L$ beaker containing $3m L$ cross-linking solution for 5 min, then taken out with the excess liquid being blown off with nitrogen, and placed in a ventilated environment for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ The gel film is stable and cannot be eroded by water.
